I successfully retrieved the ReplicaSet configuration. The flaw is clearly visible in the `spec.template.spec.containers[0].livenessProbe.httpGet.port`: it is set to `8080`. The application (Nginx) is running on port `80`.

The persistent resource owning the faulty state is the Deployment, which owns the ReplicaSet, which defines the Pod template. The Deployment definition is referenced in the ReplicaSet `ownerReferences`.

I need to update the Deployment named `app`, which is running in the `default` namespace, to change the liveness probe port from `8080` to `80`.

I will patch the Deployment.

The patch structure will target `spec.template.spec.containers[0].livenessProbe.httpGet.port`.

Deployment Name: `app`
Namespace: `default`

Patch payload:
```json
{
  "spec": {
    "template": {
      "spec": {
        "containers": [
          {
            "name": "app",
            "livenessProbe": {
              "httpGet": {
                "path": "/healthz",
                "port": 80
              },
              "failureThreshold": 1,
              "initialDelaySeconds": 1,
              "periodSeconds": 1,
              "successThreshold": 1,
              "timeoutSeconds": 1
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```
I will execute this patch. This change should cause the Deployment to roll out a new ReplicaSet, which will spawn new, correctly configured Pods, resolving the CrashLoopBackOff.

After the patch, I must confirm the original symptom is gone: The application Pods must be `Ready` and `Running`.

